---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "naked mass protein powder",
      "naked mass weight gainer",
      "vegan naked mass",
      "naked mass review",
      "naked mass gainer"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Weight Gain Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Naked Mass Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Naked Mass is a three-ingredient weight gainer, not an everyday protein powder. Here is what is in it, how the vegan version differs, whether the NSF claim holds up and what it costs per 25 g.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#whats-in-it">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#vegan-naked-mass">Vegan Naked Mass</a>
      <a href="#flavors">Calories by Flavor</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste, Mixing and Serving Size</a>
      <a href="#vs-serious-mass">Naked Mass vs Serious Mass</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Health and Who Should Buy</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Naked Mass is a solid choice for people who need a lot of extra calories and want a short ingredient list: unflavored, it gives 1,250 calories and 50 g of protein from three ingredients, per Naked. The flavor you pick changes the sugar a lot, and the serving is huge. Below we cover the label, Vegan Naked Mass, testing, cost per 25 g and who should skip it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Naked Mass Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Naked Mass is a high-calorie weight gainer with three ingredients: organic tapioca maltodextrin, whey protein concentrate and micellar casein. Per <a href="https://nakednutrition.com/products/weight-gainer-protein-supplement" rel="nofollow">Naked's product page</a> at the time of writing, a full unflavored serving gives 1,250 calories and 50 g of protein, with no sweeteners, flavors or creatine. Naked says it is Contents Certified by NSF. It is a good gainer, not a good everyday protein powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you struggle to eat enough, want gainer calories without sucralose or a long additive list, and will blend it with milk, banana or peanut butter. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you just want more protein, because roughly 80% of the calories come from carbohydrate. Skip it too if you need NSF Certified for Sport, since Naked claims the contents program, not the sport one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Naked Mass by the Numbers (unflavored, per label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1,250</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">252 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Carbohydrate per serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$3.64</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein (Amazon, 8 lb)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Naked Mass Versions and the Gainers to Compare
          </h2>

          <p>
            The two Naked Mass formulas most people search for, the most popular flavored version, and the two big-name gainers people weigh them against. Prices are what we saw on Amazon on 19 November 2026 and change often; cost per 25 g is price divided by (servings times protein per serving divided by 25).
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Naked Mass?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Unflavored Naked Mass contains only organic tapioca maltodextrin, whey protein concentrate and micellar casein, per the ingredient list on Naked's site. The only allergen it declares is milk. Naked describes it as gluten-free, soy-free and GMO-free, with no artificial sweeteners, flavors or colors, and says the whey and casein are cold processed without acid or bleach.
          </p>

          <p>
            The serving is the first thing that surprises people. Naked lists one serving as about seven scoops, or 321 g of powder, mixed into 24 oz of milk or another drink. That gives 1,250 calories, 50 g of protein, 252 g of carbohydrate, 21 g of sugars, 2.5 g of fat, 1 g of fiber, 100 mg of sodium and 75 mg of cholesterol, per the label at the time of writing. An 8 lb bag holds 11 servings.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per full serving (unflavored)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Amount, per label</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Serving size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7 scoops (321 g)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,250</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 g (whey concentrate + micellar casein)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate / sugars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">252 g / 21 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat / saturated fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.5 g / 1 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leucine / total BCAAs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.65 g / about 11.5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweeteners, creatine, added vitamins</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Maltodextrin does almost all of the calorie work. It is a starch broken down into short glucose chains, so it dissolves easily, tastes of very little and is cheap. Naked uses maltodextrin made from organic tapioca rather than corn or wheat, which it says keeps the product gluten-free. The 21 g of sugars on the label come from the maltodextrin itself, which naturally contains some short sugars; the unflavored version has no added sugar in its ingredient list.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein side is a 'fast plus slow' blend. Whey concentrate digests quickly, and micellar casein digests slowly, which is one reason Naked suggests taking Naked Mass between meals or before bed. The amino acid table on the product page lists 5.65 g of leucine and about 11.5 g of total BCAAs per serving, which is in line with what 50 g of dairy protein normally provides. If you already know Naked's single-ingredient powders from our <a href="/naked-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Naked Nutrition protein powder review</a>, this is essentially Naked Whey and Naked Casein cut with a lot of starch.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Naked Mass leaves out matters as much as what it contains. There is no creatine, no added vitamins or minerals, no digestive enzymes and no gums, which is unusual for a gainer. That makes it easy to control what else you take, but it also means you get nothing extra for the money. If you want creatine, Naked suggests adding a scoop of its own creatine; our <a href="/creatine-vs-protein-powder/">creatine vs protein powder guide</a> explains why 3 to 5 g a day is all you need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vegan-naked-mass" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is Vegan Naked Mass, and Is It as Good as the Whey Version?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vegan Naked Mass is the dairy-free version, made from organic tapioca maltodextrin, yellow pea protein and organic brown rice protein. Per <a href="https://nakednutrition.com/products/vegan-weight-gainer" rel="nofollow">Naked's vegan product page</a> at the time of writing, about six scoops (315 g) give 1,230 calories and 50 g of protein, with 11 servings in the 8 lb bag. It matches the whey version on protein and nearly matches it on calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the label is a little different. The unflavored vegan version lists 248 g of carbohydrate, 24 g of sugars, 4 g of fat, 4 g of fiber and 290 mg of sodium, against 100 mg of sodium in the dairy version. It has no cholesterol. Naked says it is vegan, gluten-free, soy-free and GMO-free, with no artificial sweeteners, flavors or colors, and that its peas come from farms in the USA and Canada.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pea and rice proteins are combined because each is lower in a different essential amino acid; together they give a complete profile. Naked's amino table lists 4.65 g of leucine per serving for the vegan version, against 5.65 g for the whey version. In practice, at 50 g of protein per serving, both deliver well over the 2 to 3 g of leucine that researchers usually treat as enough to switch on muscle building after a meal. The difference is real but small at this dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing to know before buying: Naked's vegan Mass pages, and some of its chocolate dairy Mass pages, carry a California Proposition 65 warning that the product can expose you to chemicals including lead. That is a disclosure, not a test result, and it is common for plant proteins and cacao, which pick up trace minerals from soil. Naked separately says the vegan version gets independent third-party testing for heavy metals. If lead exposure is a particular worry, for example during pregnancy, ask a doctor or registered dietitian and see our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder guide</a> for alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            So is it as good? For anyone who cannot have dairy, yes: it is one of very few big gainers with a short, plant-only ingredient list. Buyers in Naked's review summary say it is gentle on the stomach and that many people with lactose intolerance use it as their go-to, while taste gets more mixed comments than the whey version. If you only want plant protein and not 1,200 calories, a regular vegan powder such as the one in our <a href="/blessed-protein-powder-review/">Blessed protein powder review</a> or a <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">pea protein</a> fits better.
          </p>
