        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Naked Mass Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, according to Naked: both Naked Mass and Vegan Naked Mass are labeled 'Contents Certified by NSF' on Naked's product pages, and the Amazon titles say 'NSF Certified'. Naked explains that this means the contents are tested for accuracy and purity and confirmed to have no harmful levels of contaminants, including heavy metals and pesticides.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is a different program from NSF Certified for Sport. NSF Contents Certified checks that what is on the label is in the tub and that contaminants are within limits. NSF Certified for Sport adds screening for substances banned in sport and lot-by-lot monitoring, and it is the mark most college and professional athletes are told to look for. We found no claim from Naked that Naked Mass holds the sport certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also tried to confirm the certificate ourselves. When we searched <a href="https://info.nsf.org/Certified/Dietary/Listings.asp?CompanyName=naked&Standard=173" rel="nofollow">NSF's public dietary supplement listings</a> on 19 November 2026, the entry for Naked Whey, Inc. showed Naked Energy and Naked Creatine, but not Naked Mass. That listing may not include every Contents Certified product, so this does not mean the claim is false. It does mean the certification rests on Naked's word for now, and we have recorded it that way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every flavor's Amazon title mentions NSF either. The unflavored, vanilla and chocolate whey listings and the unflavored and vanilla vegan listings do; the chocolate vegan, chocolate peanut butter and strawberry listings we saw did not. If certification is your main reason for buying, stick to the versions whose label shows the mark, and see our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF-certified protein powder guide</a> for powders whose status you can look up directly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">FOR TESTED ATHLETES</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Contents Certified is not Certified for Sport</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you are drug tested, do not rely on a contents or quality claim. Look the exact product up on nsfsport.com or the Informed Sport or Informed Choice databases before you buy. If it is not there, choose a gainer or protein that is.</p>
          </div>
