          <p>
            Buyers mostly like Naked Mass, and the most common complaint is the size of the serving. Naked's site showed a 4.9 out of 5 average from 1,563 reviews of the dairy version and 1,365 reviews of the vegan version at the time of writing, with an AI-generated summary of the 100 most recent reviews. We have not tasted it ourselves; what follows is what buyers report.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Taste and texture</h3>

          <p>
            For the whey version, buyers say it tastes good and mixes well without being chalky or thick, and the chocolate and vanilla flavors are singled out as the most popular. The unflavored version tastes of very little, so buyers treat it as a base and add milk, fruit, oats, cocoa or peanut butter. Vegan Naked Mass gets more divided comments: some buyers like the chocolate, while others call it bland or gritty, though many like that the unflavored version takes on whatever it is blended with.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Digestion</h3>

          <p>
            Many reviewers of both versions say it does not cause the bloating they had with other gainers, which they put down to the short ingredient list. That is a pattern in buyer comments, not something a label can prove, and a 1,250-calorie shake can still be a lot for one sitting. Buyers who report weight gain often mention gaining 10 to 30 lb over weeks or months alongside training, per the summary.
