          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Naked Mass when you wanted Naked Whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The bags look alike, but Naked Mass is mostly maltodextrin: 252 g of carbohydrate and 1,250 calories per serving of the unflavored version. If you wanted protein to hit a daily target without changing your weight, Naked Whey gives 25 g of protein for about 120 calories, per its label, and costs far less per 25 g.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting with the full seven-scoop serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jumping straight to 321 g of powder in one shake is the fastest route to the bloating and fullness buyers complain about. Start with half a serving, roughly 625 calories, for a week or two. If the scale has not moved, add a second half serving later in the day rather than drinking one giant shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every flavor has the same sugar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unflavored Naked Mass lists 21 g of sugars per serving. Chocolate and Vanilla list 58 g, and Strawberry lists 98 g, because they add organic coconut sugar. If you picked Naked Mass to keep sugar down, the flavor choice matters more than the brand choice. Check the panel on the exact bag.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating NSF Contents Certified as sport-safe</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Naked states that Naked Mass is Contents Certified by NSF, which covers label accuracy and contaminants. It is not the same as NSF Certified for Sport, which screens for banned substances. Drug-tested athletes should look the exact product up on nsfsport.com and choose a sport-certified option if it is not listed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling up on calories from the shake and the mixer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Seven scoops in 24 oz of whole milk adds roughly 450 calories and 24 g of protein from the milk alone, on top of the 1,250 in the powder. That can be helpful, but it can also overshoot. Count the liquid and anything else you blend in before deciding how much powder you need.
              </p>
